Pi-DOS Setup
============

Run PIDOSCFG at the DOS prompt. Click the CPU, Memory, Video, Sound, Mixer,
Input and System tabs, or use F5/F6. Tab/Shift+Tab moves focus; Enter edits a field.
Up/Down or Home/End chooses a CPU profile or RAM size. Other lists support
typing to search, arrows, Home/End and PgUp/PgDn. Click an item to select it.
Accept (F2 or Alt+A; Alt+S also works) saves ALL pages and exits.
Cancel (Escape, Alt+C or Alt+X) discards edits and exits, restoring unsaved
mixer preview. These are the only two main buttons. F3 or Alt+R restores
current-page defaults. Reboot Pi-DOS to activate hardware, Turbo configuration,
shortcuts and other settings. Mixer preview stays live, and an already
configured Turbo shortcut switches speed immediately. There is no F4 Apply.

System's Enable VGA666 checkbox selects GPIO VGA output (default off).
Accept and cleanly REBOOT or POWEROFF to update BOOT for the next start.
CRT/LCD remains a separate monitor setting.
VGA666 uses GPIO 2-21, disables the header UART, and carries no audio.
Select USB or supported analogue audio. To return to HDMI, uncheck, Accept
and cleanly restart. Matching new Image and BOOT assets are required.

The CPU profile sets normal speed. Turbo below the profiles offers only higher
MHz presets from the selected family (and Disabled):
  286:     8, 12, 16, 20, 25
  386DX:   16, 20, 25, 33, 40
  486:     25, 33, 50, 66, 75, 100, 120, 133
  Pentium: 60, 66, 75, 90, 100, 120, 133, 150, 166, 200
Turbo starts disabled. Select a speed, Accept and reboot; Ctrl+Alt+T toggles speeds
without changing CPU model/core. System can change the shortcut. A two-second
top-right notification appears on CRT and LCD, independent of the CPU HUD.
Automatic has no fixed-MHz Turbo choices. Windows settings save these fields
for the next Pi-DOS startup; its existing live Volume panel is unchanged.

Restore defaults (F3 or Alt+R) resets the CURRENT page, pending Accept.
A mouse is optional. Enter activates the focused button.

System / Monitor type: CRT (default) or LCD.
LCD keeps a fixed output: 640x480, 800x600, 832x624, 1024x768 (default),
1280x720 or 1920x1080. Choose 4:3 (default) or Original pixels aspect;
the picture fits the screen with black borders as needed. Original pixels
preserves the DOS raster ratio, for example 320x200 is 8:5.
Linear filtering smooths LCD scaling using SDL2; disabled by default.
CPU and temperature overlay shows host core load and CPU/SoC temperature.
It is LCD-only, off by default, independent of logs, and takes effect on reboot.
LCD options are hidden in CRT mode and retained when switching back.
CRT follows DOS resolutions and refresh rates without software effects.
Existing files without a monitor setting keep their previous display setup.
Select a monitor type, Accept and REBOOT to apply the managed profile.

System / Audio output: HDMI (default), 3.5 mm jack (Pi 3/4), or External
USB DAC. Accept and REBOOT to apply. HDMI uses the primary HDMI port.
Connect a USB audio-class DAC before boot. With multiple USB outputs,
the first ALSA card ID alphabetically is used, then its lowest playback
device number. An unavailable output stays silent for that boot.

RAM: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (default), 32, 64 or 128 MiB. Above 16 MiB can break
older programs; some need less. The selected CPU must address that RAM.
Enable XMS and Enable EMS are independent checkboxes below the RAM sizes.
Both start enabled. Tab to a checkbox and press Space or Enter, or click it.
Changing RAM leaves these switches unchanged. Accept saves both switches;
reboot Pi-DOS to activate changes. Restore defaults
on Memory selects 16 MiB and enables both. Existing advanced EMS modes stay
unchanged until edited; re-enabling EMS selects the standard enabled mode.

Video: use the same selection list as Sound to choose IBM CGA, Hercules,
Tandy, IBM EGA, IBM VGA, Tseng Labs ET4000, S3 Trio64 (default), VESA VBE 3.0
or VESA VBE 2.0. The VESA options use DOSBox-X's virtual graphics adapter.
The utility also supports Hercules monochrome text mode.
The 3dfx Voodoo checkbox is checked by default. Checked enables software
Voodoo with 12 MiB of video memory; unchecked disables the hardware.
Toggling it saves voodoo_card=software (or false), voodoo_maxmem=true and
glide=false. Glide passthrough stays disabled. Accept and reboot Pi-DOS for video changes.

Sound: None, Tandy sound, AdLib, Creative Game Blaster/CMS, Sound Blaster
2.0, Sound Blaster Pro 2 (default), Sound Blaster 16, or GUS Classic.
Choose matching port/IRQ/DMA in the game's setup. Applicable controls
include SB16 high DMA, FM mode, and GUS sample RAM/patch directory.
AdLib and Tandy ports are fixed. GUS patches are not bundled; some games
need patch files under C:\ULTRASND\MIDI. Selecting a preset disables other
managed cards. MIDI remains independent. PC speaker is separately enabled
by default; PC Speaker is the first control and is checked when enabled.

MIDI output is independent: None, Roland SC-55 (default), Roland GM,
Roland MT-32, Roland CM-32L or External USB MIDI. SC-55 and Roland GM use
the supplied GM banks; MT-32/CM-32L use Munt with control and PCM ROMs.
Internal MIDI plays through the same selected output as the sound card.
Choose the corresponding music device in each game's setup.

MIDI settings: MPU-401 Intelligent (default) or UART, port 330h (default)
and automatic IRQ (default). For USB MIDI, choose the connected destination
and optional SysEx delay for older modules. F5 or Rescan refreshes the
destination list. Selections survive ALSA client-number changes; keep
interfaces without a unique serial number on the same USB socket.
Connect external modules' audio outputs to your own speakers or mixer.
Internal MIDI volume does not control an external module's audio.
Assets are in C:\SYSTEM\MIDI: SC55.SF2, GMROLAND.SF2, MT32 and CM32L.
Missing assets/devices disable MIDI for that boot and appear in DOS MIDI.
Accept and reboot after changing MIDI output or interface settings.

Mixer: left:right percentages from 0=mute to 100=normal maximum, including
CD audio from images and USB drives. Only present mixer channels are shown.
Use left/right arrows or [-]/[+] for 5% steps, or Enter for a preset
or custom stereo value. Active channels apply IMMEDIATELY when the selected
sound hardware matches the running setup. The page shows LIVE or AFTER
REBOOT. Cancel restores starting live levels at whole-percent precision;
Accept keeps them and saves edited levels for later boots. Hardware changes
suspend preview and restore the original live levels. Inactive channels
and unavailable live control take effect after reboot. Live queries need
a writable C:\TEMP or C:\. Temporary query files are removed automatically.
Internal MIDI music controls FluidSynth/Munt alongside the other channels.

System: search keyboard layouts, DOS countries and installed timezones.
Keyboard, country and timezone are independent. Defaults are Spanish,
Spain and Europe/Madrid. Daylight saving follows the selected timezone.
Custom country values can include a code page, e.g. 1,437. Timezones must
be in the installed list. Diagnostic logs is unchecked by default; check
it to capture boot/runtime/network/video logs in C:\SYSTEM after reboot.
These settings take effect after REBOOT.

First boot:
  Setup keeps the active 80x25 text mode and page, avoiding a CRT reset.
  Screens use BIOS text output for reliable redraws.
  Fresh installations ask for keyboard, country, timezone and CRT/LCD.
  LCD also offers a fixed resolution. F2 saves and reboots cleanly.
  Esc skips, discards pending edits, and keeps the saved defaults.
  After reboot, test typing and review the selected local date/time.
  Enter edits date/time; F2 or Esc finishes with the current clock.
  Manual clock changes affect this DOS session only. DATE /S resumes
  host-time synchronization after a manual adjustment.
  Settings remain editable on System; DATE and TIME adjust the clock.
  Existing installations and read-only/recovery boots are not interrupted.
  PIDOSCFG /FIRSTBOOT reopens welcome or the pending clock review.
  When opened manually, run REBOOT after saving regional settings.
  State in [pidos]: first_boot=pending, then clock, then done.
  Failed saves keep setup pending. Normal page resets keep this state.

Profiles:
  Automatic
  286 12 MHz
  386DX 33 MHz         Pi 2/3 default
  486DX 33 MHz
  486DX2 66 MHz         Pi 4 default
  486DX4 100 MHz
  Pentium 75 MHz
  Pentium 100 MHz
  Pentium 120 MHz       Pi 5 default

Fresh images select the board's CPU before DOS starts, including when welcome
is skipped. The CPU tab marks all three defaults and recommends this board's
profile. Restore defaults selects that recommendation; Accept saves it. Reboot to activate.
Updates and moving the card to another board preserve saved CPU settings.
Pi 2/3/Zero 2/CM3 use 386DX/33; Pi 4/400/CM4/CM4S use 486DX2/66;
Pi 5/500/CM5 use Pentium 120. Defaults reflect reported Pi-DOS hardware tests.

MHz labels describe approximate performance equivalents, not exact clocks.
The Raspberry Pi must be fast enough to sustain the requested speed.
At boot, Time appears directly below Date on the left.
The right column lists System, DOS CPU, DOS memory, DOS video, DOS Voodoo,
DOS sound and DOS MIDI. DOS Voodoo displays Enabled/Disabled.
Keyboard, country and timezone are on the Regional line below the PI-DOS
storage and Drives lines.

Saving updates only edited settings in C:\SYSTEM\PIDOS.CFG. Other settings
and comments on separate lines are preserved. Custom settings remain until
changed. Run REBOOT or press Ctrl+Alt+Delete after saving hardware or
regional changes. The shortcut reboots the Pi and reloads saved settings;
save application data first.
The original file is backed up as PIDOS.BAK. Saving again replaces that
backup with the immediately previous version. Read-only files are left
unchanged. Files over 24 KiB cannot be edited.

If PIDOS.TMP exists after an interrupted save, check it and PIDOS.BAK before
deleting or renaming recovery files. Setup will not overwrite an existing
PIDOS.TMP. You can restore PIDOS.BAK to PIDOS.CFG from another computer.

Command-line examples:
  PIDOSCFG /LIST
  PIDOSCFG /PROFILE 486-66
  PIDOSCFG /CONFIG C:\SYSTEM\TEST.CFG
  PIDOSCFG /HELP

Program and source are original Pi-DOS work. Built with Open Watcom.
For the compiler runtime license and source information, see WATCOM.TXT
in this directory.

Input tab: select DOS joystick type, automatic/raw controller layout,
left stick/D-pad, button order, dead zone, timing and autofire. Games
calibrate and assign DOS joystick controls in their own setup. Controllers
can connect after boot or reconnect without a system reboot. Some games
only detect/calibrate controllers during game startup. No mapper file needed.

System tab: reboot, poweroff and pause accept None, Ctrl+Alt+Delete
or Ctrl+Alt+F1-F10.
Reboot defaults to Ctrl+Alt+Delete; poweroff and pause are disabled by default.
Duplicate shortcuts cannot be saved. Repeat pause or press Escape to resume.
Accept saves shortcuts and other Input/System settings. REBOOT activates them.

NETWORK
-------
Network configures IPX tunnel multiplayer, NE2000 with SLIRP TCP/IP or PCAP
wired LAN, DOS driver/DHCP/static startup, and Windows for Workgroups drivers.
Accept and REBOOT. Run NETUP to retry, NETINFO for status, or WFWNET before WIN.
Windows 3.11 real LAN sharing uses PCAP and Windows' own NE2000 NDIS driver.
Windows/TCP-IP-32 media and native ODI/IPX drivers must be supplied separately.
Read C:\DOCS\NETWORK.TXT or Z:\NETWORK\NETWORK.TXT for full instructions.
